Take $A=1$. By assumption $E=(1\downarrow F)$ has <finite limits>, so $E^{\mathrm{op}}$ has finite <colimits>. A <filtered category> is nonempty, admits a cocone over every pair of objects, and admits a coequalizing arrow for every parallel pair. The <initial object> of $E^{\mathrm{op}}$ gives nonemptiness, its binary <coproducts> give the required two-object cocones, and its <coequalizers> equalize parallel arrows. Consequently $\boxed{\text{(ii)}\Rightarrow\text{(iii)}}$. These statements also handle the otherwise possible empty <category of elements>.

= iii
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Every covariant <representable functor> preserves <categorical limits>: arrows from its representing object into a limiting object are exactly compatible cones of arrows into the diagram. Apply the density formula proved in the root solution. For any finite diagram $D:J\to\mathcal C$,
$$
F(\lim_JD)\cong\operatorname*{colim}_{e\in E^{\mathrm{op}}}\lim_{j\in J}H_e(D_j)
\cong\lim_{j\in J}\operatorname*{colim}_{e\in E^{\mathrm{op}}}H_e(D_j)
\cong\lim_J FD.
$$
The middle <isomorphism> uses exactly the permitted theorem that <filtered colimits> commute with <finite limits> in sets; it applies because $E^{\mathrm{op}}$ is filtered. All maps are the natural comparison maps, so $F$ preserves <finite limits>, including the <terminal object>. This proves $\boxed{\text{(iii)}\Rightarrow\text{(i)}}$ and completes the equivalence.

