{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Assume both unit and counit are monic. The unit criterion makes $F$ faithful. The triangle identity is $\varepsilon_{FA}F\eta_A=1_{FA}$. Since $\varepsilon_{FA}$ is monic, this <split epimorphism> is an <isomorphism>: cancelling it in $\varepsilon_{FA}(F\eta_A\varepsilon_{FA})=\varepsilon_{FA}$ gives $F\eta_A\varepsilon_{FA}=1$. Thus $F\eta_A$ is invertible. As $\mathcal C$ is balanced, the reflection result in the root solution makes $\eta_A$ invertible. Hence $F$ is full and faithful.

Let $q:FA\to B$ be a <regular epimorphism>. Its adjunct factorization is $q=\varepsilon_BFh$ for $h:A\to GB$. A <regular epimorphism> is extremal: if a <coequalizer> $q$ factors through a <monomorphism> $m$, its other factor also coequalizes the defining pair, descends through $q$, and gives a right inverse for $m$; monicity then makes $m$ invertible. Apply this to the monic $\varepsilon_B$. More explicitly, if $q$ coequalizes $r,s$, monicity gives $Fh r=Fh s$, so there is $d:B\to FGB$ with $dq=Fh$. Then $\varepsilon_Bdq=q$ implies $\varepsilon_Bd=1$, and monicity gives $d\varepsilon_B=1$. Therefore $B\cong FGB$. The image is closed under regular quotients, proving $\boxed{\text{(i)}\Rightarrow\text{(ii)}}$.

= ii
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Assume $F$ is full and faithful and its image is closed under regular quotients. Its unit is invertible, hence monic. Factor the counit using the hypothesis on $\mathcal D$:
$$
FGB\overset{e}{\twoheadrightarrow}I\overset{m}{\hookrightarrow}B,
$$
with $e$ regular epic and $m$ monic. Closure gives $I\cong FA'$ for some $A'$. A right adjoint preserves <monomorphisms>: postcomposition with $Gm$ on $\mathcal C(X,-)$ corresponds under <adjunction> to postcomposition with $m$ on $\mathcal D(FX,-)$, which is injective. Thus $Gm$ is monic.

The triangle identity and invertibility of $\eta_{GB}$ give $G\varepsilon_B=(\eta_{GB})^{-1}$, so $Gm\,Ge$ is invertible. A <monomorphism> whose composite with another arrow is an <isomorphism> is itself split epic and hence an <isomorphism>; therefore both $Gm$ and $Ge$ are <isomorphisms>. The arrow $e$ is between objects in the essential image of the full and faithful $F$. After choosing $I\cong FA'$, write it as $Fh$. Naturality of the invertible unit identifies $GFh$ with $h$, so $Ge$ invertible implies $h$ and hence $e$ invertible. Now $\varepsilon_B=me$ is monic. This proves $\boxed{\text{(ii)}\Rightarrow\text{(i)}}$.

